---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---
<PageLayout
  title="Safety and Intended Use"
  seoTitle="Safety and Intended Use of the Golden Moments App"
  description="Golden Moments is a recreational picture activity, not a dementia test or treatment. How to use picture games respectfully with an older adult."
  path="/safety/"
  crumb="Safety and intended use"
  updated="2026-10-25"
  lede="A pleasant moment is enough. No one needs to remember a name, get an answer right or finish a game."
>
  <aside class="notice" aria-labelledby="safety-summary">
    <div>
      <strong id="safety-summary">Before you play</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Golden Moments is for <strong>recreation and shared conversation</strong>.</li>
        <li>It is <strong>not intended for medical diagnosis, screening, treatment or cognitive assessment</strong>.</li>
        <li>Let the person choose what feels enjoyable. Stop or change the activity whenever they wish.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <h2>What the app and website offer</h2>
  <p>
    Golden Moments offers picture matching and other activities to look at and talk about together. Families and
    caregivers may share them with an older adult, including someone living with dementia or Alzheimer’s disease, if
    that person enjoys them. People can also enjoy the pictures on their own.
  </p>
  <p>The purpose is enjoyment, familiarity and time together, not a score or a clinical outcome.</p>

  <h2>What they are not designed to do</h2>
  <p>
    Golden Moments does not diagnose, screen for, assess, monitor, treat, slow or prevent dementia, Alzheimer’s disease or
    any other illness. It does not give therapy, medical guidance, medication advice, emergency help or a care plan.
    Describing it as recreational is not a regulatory clearance or classification.
  </p>
  <p>
    How quickly someone finds a match, or whether they recognize a picture, is not a measure of their intelligence,
    capacity, health or disease stage. Do not use game activity to make health or care decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep it voluntary</h2>
  <p>
    Ask if the person would like to look at pictures. Keep it simple and offer choices. Let them enjoy a picture without
    needing its name or the year. If a story comes naturally, listen. If not, quiet company counts too.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Stop, take a break, or change activities</strong> if someone becomes tired, uncomfortable, confused, upset or
    uninterested. A familiar picture can bring back an unhappy memory, so never insist on it. Give the everyday
    supervision and support the person needs; the app does not replace it.
  </p>

  <h2>Comfort and accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    Use a comfortable screen brightness and a position that makes pictures easy to see, along with the device’s
    accessibility settings. There is no need to make a game harder or time it. If sounds bother someone, keep sound off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read how we design the website on our <a href="/accessibility/">accessibility page</a>. Device compatibility for the
    app is listed in the App Store and Google Play.
  </p>

  <h2>Family photos and consent</h2>
  <p>
    Choose only photos you may use and show. Take special care with photos of children or of anyone who cannot easily say
    what they want. Do not share another person’s diagnosis or other private details as part of an activity.
  </p>
  <p>
    Original photos, the app’s copies and device backups are different things. Read the
    <a href="/privacy-choices/">deletion steps</a> and the <a href="/app-privacy/">app privacy policy</a> before adding
    family photos.
  </p>

  <h2>Medical care and emergencies</h2>
  <p>
    If you are worried about someone’s memory, behavior, safety or health, talk with a doctor or other qualified
    professional. Golden Moments cannot advise on one person’s condition or care. If someone is in danger or has a
    medical emergency, call 911 or your local emergency number. <strong>Do not rely on the app for urgent help.</strong>
    Our <a href="/medical-disclaimer/">health information notice</a> lists helplines.
  </p>

  <h2>Report a concern</h2>
  <p>
    If you find an upsetting picture, misleading wording, an accessibility barrier or a safety issue, email
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}?subject=Golden%20Moments%20safety%20concern`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>. Describe
    the screen or feature. Please leave out health details and family photos unless they are truly needed.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
